WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.870
Galileo Galilei was a truth seeker.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:23.410
His quest for truth, though, was to bring
him into conflict with the most powerful institution

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.470
of his day – the Roman Catholic Church.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:31.460
Through such inventions as the telescope,
he revealed truths that landed him in front

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.730
of the Holy Roman inquisition, fighting for
his very survival.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.420
Condemned during his life, he is now revered
as a true genius.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:48.470
In this today’s Biographics, we get the
full story on Galileo Galilei.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:54.900
Beginnings
Galileo Galilei was born on February 15th,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.329
1564 in Pisa, Italy.

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:02.320
He was the first of six children to Vincenzo
de Bonajuti de Galilei and Giula Galilei.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.460
The Galilee’s were from an ancient, respected
family who originated in Florence.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.580
Vincenzo was a wool trader who also had an
abiding interest in philosophy, science and

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:13.580
music.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.390
At the age of four days, young Galileo was
baptized a Catholic.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.670
He was raised according to the strict tenets
of that faith, as was everyone else in the

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.670
region.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.679
From an early age, Galileo showed an intense
interest in making things.

00:01:25.679 --> 00:01:30.860
He was constantly making small gadgets that
would amuse his friends and teachers.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.490
These would include wooden animals with moving
arms and legs.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:39.459
He was fascinated with how machines worked
and would be constantly pulling things apart

00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:40.850
and putting them back together.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:46.459
If he was making a machine and didn’t have
a part, he would improvise.

00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:50.930
When Galileo was ten years of age, his father
decided to move the family to Florence.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.119
By now, Vincenzo had developed a reputation
as a learned and wise man.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:58.530
Despite the demands of business, he had managed
to write several books, the most popular of

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.560
which was the Dialogue of Ancient and Modern
Music.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.399
Wealthy people streamed to him to benefit
from his knowledge of Greek and Roman literature

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.259
and to hear his musical theories.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:13.300
He was invited to spend time with Duke Albrecht
of Bavaria, who lived in Munich, Germany,

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.720
some 300 miles from Florence.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.690
Vincenzo was also an active member of the
Florentine Camerata, a group of musicians

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:26.110
who were experimenting with musical drama,
the genesis of what would be opera.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:31.459
Galileo and his siblings were, therefore,
raised in an intellectually stimulating environment.

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:35.319
Vincenzo taught his oldest son how to play
a number of musical instruments, the favorite

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:38.150
of which, for both, was the lute.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:43.540
Galileo also experimented with drawing and
painting, showing great promise in both areas.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:49.019
His teenage years coincided with the high
Renaissance period, when Italy was being filled

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:53.140
with the works of the great painters, sculptors,
and architects.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.280
He considered devoting his life to that of
an artist, but his father was keen for his

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.480
son to follow him into the wool trade.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.840
Vincenzo had a great passion for the arts,
but he knew that nothing but a solid business

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.450
career would secure for his son a stable financial
future.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:12.120
By the time that Galileo had reached his mid-teens
it was apparent to everyone, including his

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.450
father, that his keen mind was destined for
greater things than a life as a wool trader.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.270
Vincenzo saw it as his duty to encourage Galileo
to use his God-given intellectual talents

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.390
to the fullest extent.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:29.469
Through his studies of the works of Aristotle,
Vincenzo had instilled in his son the discipline

00:03:29.469 --> 00:03:33.530
of independent study, observation and experimentation.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.969
Rather than being swayed by what others had
already concluded on a matter, he would independently

00:03:37.969 --> 00:03:42.349
study the subject for himself and reach his
own conclusions based on those observations.

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:46.290
When he was fifteen years of age, Galileo
attended the Benedictine Monastery of Santa

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.080
Maria di Vallombrosa.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.640
From the monks he learned religion and logic.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.780
After a few months, however, he had to return
home due to an eye infection.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:02.569
Higher Education
When he was 17, Galileo passed the bachelor’s

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:05.810
exam, permitting entry to the University of
Pisa.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:09.989
Universities in the 16th century were places
attended by members of only the wealthiest

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:15.810
families and were generally reserved for those
with ambitions of being priests, doctors or

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:16.810
professors.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.830
Although Galileo seriously considered the
priesthood as a young man, Galileo, or more

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:24.740
likely Galileo’s father, had decided on
a career in medicine.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:28.400
In order to achieve it, he first had to obtain
a master’s degree.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.460
Galileo applied himself to his studies and
made excellent progress.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:35.380
However, several months into the first year
of his medical degree, he happened to overhear

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:39.850
a geometry lecture delivered by a friend of
his father, Ostilio Ricci.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:44.680
He was immediately attracted to the logic
and beauty of the math that was being applied.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.230
When Ricci heard from Galileo’s father that
the boy was fascinated with his lecture, he

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:49.770
invited him to take his course.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.810
Vincenzo had first made Ricci promise not
to let Galileo know that he had consented

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:59.120
to him taking the geometry paper in case he
saw it as permission to abandon his medical

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.120
studies.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.810
In addition to attending Geometry lectures,
Galileo worked one-on-one with Ricci as he

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.070
studied the works of Greek mathematician Euclid.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:11.410
As he become more deeply immersed in the subject,
Galileo became less interested in medicine.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.930
Finally, he confronted his father and asked
him not to stand in the way of his focus on

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:16.930
geometry.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.640
Vincenzo was a realist, knowing that his son
would not prosper as a doctor if his heart

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.640
was not in it.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.640
He agreed to support Galileo in his mathematical
pursuits.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:33.060
Galileo the Mathematician
After switching to mathematics, Galileo brought

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.410
his full intellectual rigor to bear on the
subject.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.450
He was an excellent student, but he still
managed to frustrate his professors.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:44.400
The inclination to question everything that
his father had instilled in him meant that

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.780
he would accept nothing as fact.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.650
Established theories were meaningless until
he had personally pulled them apart, studies

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.380
them and reached his own conclusions.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.710
It was the Aristotelian scientific method
in action, but it even caused the teenager

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:00.780
to question the theories of Aristotle himself.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.300
One day when he was 19, Galileo happened to
be in the Pisa Cathedral for a mass observance.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.030
After the service he was wandering around
the building when his attention was caught

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:12.441
by a sculpted bronze lamp located in the dome
of the cathedral.

00:06:12.441 --> 00:06:14.690
The lamp was swinging back and forth like
a pendulum.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:19.580
He observed that, regardless of the lessening
arc of the swing, the time that it took for

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.440
each swing remained the same.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.460
To check this observation, he timed the swings
with his pulse.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.900
This led him to the realization that the rhythmic
swings of a pendulum were an excellent way

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.970
to measure the human pulse.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.930
This was the inspiration for the later invention
of Galileo’s pulsilogium, a machine designed

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.880
to take people’s pulses, or a harmonic oscillator.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:42.090
This was the first example of Galileo not
only observing and testing phenomena but also

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.320
finding practical applications of his findings.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:53.050
Practical Applications
By 1585, the financial strain of supporting

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:57.960
his son’s university studies was too much
for Vincenzo and Galileo had to withdraw from

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.560
the University of Pisa.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.590
He hadn’t yet completed his doctoral degree.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.710
But the budding genius was not to be deterred.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:08.710
He embarked upon his own studies by examining
the works of the great minds of Ancient Rome

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:09.710
and Greece.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.810
After dissecting the works of Archimedes,
he wrote his first scientific dissertation,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:17.220
in which he described an invention that he
called ‘the little balance’.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:21.259
This was a hydrostatic scale to measure the
air/water balance.

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:25.310
He also developed a horse-powered pump for
crop irrigation that was inspired by the famous

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.900
Archimedes Screw.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:32.090
Just as his father had a decade earlier with
his muscle talent, Galileo began to impress

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:35.240
important people with his practical application
of science.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.270
He gained a reputation as an up and coming
scientist.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:42.190
One man who was especially interested in Galileo’s
work was the Marquis Guidobaldo del Monte,

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:46.540
a wealthy patron with an abiding interest
in astronomy, mathematics and architecture.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:50.860
The Marquis began writing to Galileo and the
two maintained a vigorous correspondence during

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:54.970
which Galileo would explain his latest observations
and applications.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.280
Del Monte encouraged the twenty-two-year-old
Galileo to study the center of gravity in

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:04.070
solid bodies, which led to the publication
of a treatise on the subject.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:10.450
University Professor
It was through del Monte that Galileo came

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:14.870
to the attention of the Grand Duke of Tuscany,
Ferdinand I de’ Medici.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:18.960
In 1589, the Grand Duke appointed him as a
professor of mathematics at the University

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:19.960
of Pisa.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.960
This was the position that Galileo had been
hoping to secure even since he had been forced

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:24.960
to quit his studies.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.590
At the time, mathematics was a minor subject
of study at university that was not even seen

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:31.780
as worthy of study by many.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:37.721
Galileo’s salary was only 60 florins a year,
while philosophy professors earned 600 or

00:08:37.721 --> 00:08:38.721
more.

00:08:38.721 --> 00:08:43.310
In addition, he was looked down on by them
as a young upstart who dared to question the

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:45.690
teachings of the great Aristotle.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:49.820
To them this was akin to blasphemy, with the
writings of Aristotle being considered the

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.350
final word on all things.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:57.920
Ironically, Galileo was simply following through
on the teachings of Aristotle himself, who

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.640
championed independent study and observation,
well aware that his own theories could well

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.800
be overturned as new knowledge was observed.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.900
The most famous example of Galileo not simply
accepting one of Aristotle’s theories because

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:13.860
it was written down on paper had to do with
the speed of falling objects.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.720
Aristotle had contended that two objects of
different weights would fall to the ground

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:21.590
at different speeds, with the heaviest object
falling faster.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:26.121
As the story goes, Galileo climbed to the
top of the Leaning Tower of Pisa, although

00:09:26.121 --> 00:09:30.340
the building thought to have been used may
have been the church tower in Delft, and dropped

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:33.520
two spheres of different masses to the ground
below.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.150
They both hit the ground at the same time,
disproving Aristotle’s theory.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:41.650
Galileo’s experiment at the Tower of Pisa
was believed to have been observed by many

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:44.750
of his students and fellow professors from
the University.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:48.820
Still, some of the professors refused to acknowledge
that the young man had just overturned one

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.440
of Aristotle’ theories.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:55.150
This stubborn refusal to accept empirical
evidence that overturned long entrenched views

00:09:55.150 --> 00:10:26.520
is something that Galileo was to contend with
for the rest of

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:44.580
his life.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:11:22.290
Making Enemies
Not long after the Tower experiment, Galileo

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:26.930
was approached by a member of the powerful
Medici family, Giovanni de’ Medici, and

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.030
asked to evaluate a machine that he had invented.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:33.030
The machine was designed to dredge harbor
bottoms of Leghorn to remove the mucky layer

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.410
and make them safer for ships to come in.

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:39.270
Galileo carefully studied the machine, only
to conclude that it was badly designed and

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.270
was not fit for purpose.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:42.550
This presented a problem.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:46.660
Should he be honest and tell the man that
the machine wouldn’t work or should he placate

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:48.620
him with false praise?

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:53.540
The Medici’s were the most powerful clan
in Italy and Galileo was very wary of getting

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:55.240
on the wrong side of any of them.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:00.050
Still, his loyalty to scientific truth would
not allow him to deceive the inventor.

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:03.880
In a public forum, he spelled out what was
wrong with the machine and assured everyone

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.440
present that it would not work.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:10.860
His opinion was borne out when the machine
proved to be a dismal failure.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:13.750
Galileo’s honesty had created a powerful
enemy.

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:17.839
The humiliated Medici man was determined to
bring Galileo down and set about getting him

00:12:17.839 --> 00:12:19.840
fired from his professorship at the university.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:25.050
Under the advice of the Marquis Guidobaldo
del Monte, Galileo resigned his position.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:29.270
Del Monte had opened the door to a new posting,
as the chair of mathematics at the University

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:32.620
of Padua, 130 miles northeast of Pisa.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.630
This was a big step up for Galileo.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:38.720
He was now making 180 florins a year, three
times his previous income.

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:41.690
No longer did he have to contend with the
ridicule of his fellow professors.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.450
He soon became a very popular lecturer with
his classes being fill to overflowing.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:49.060
At Padua, he also found the time to work on
his inventions and his writings.

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:53.490
He became an expert at applying science to
military fortifications, designing reinforced

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:57.150
walls for forts and mechanically superior
siege engines.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:03.360
In 1593, was invited to Venice to give advice
on the ideal positioning of a rowing ship’s

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.400
oars to generate maximum use of manpower.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:11.030
In 1593, Galileo developed an undiagnosed
illness which had kept him in pain, affected

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:13.260
his appetite and robbed him of sleep.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:16.130
These symptoms would affect him for the rest
of his life.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.980
Despite his sickness he managed to meet his
university obligations.

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:23.570
At the end of his first six-year term at Padua,
he was appointed for a further six years.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:28.100
By the end of the 1590’s, Galileo had invented
a range of products which were in demand all

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:29.160
over the country.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.950
These included an irrigation pump that require
a single horse to power it and the sector

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.270
which was used to measure precise angles.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:40.830
Lacking the time to produce each piece himself,
he hired a craftsman named Marcantonio Mazzoleni

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.460
to make his inventions.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:51.020
Galileo the Astronomer
It wasn’t until the mid-1590’s that Galileo

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.350
took any serious interest in astronomy.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:57.130
Without studying the issue, he believed in
the Ptolemaic theory that the sun and other

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.230
planets revolved around the earth.

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:02.950
When a visiting lecturer spoke on the Copernican
theory, which contended that the earth and

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:07.360
other planets actually revolve around the
sun, Galileo did not attend, considering the

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:08.570
theory to be ridiculous.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:12.779
Same time later, he was talking with some
of his students who had attended the lecture.

00:14:12.779 --> 00:14:16.750
Most of them didn’t accept the new theory
but there was one who did.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:21.899
It was then that Galileo had a simple but
profound realization – every person who

00:14:21.899 --> 00:14:26.330
had abandoned the Ptolemaic theory in favor
of the Copernican theory would be subjected

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:27.330
to ridicule.

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:30.960
They would, therefore, only do so on the basis
of overwhelming evidence.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:35.300
He was now obsessed with finding what that
evidence was.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:39.540
The question of whether the sun or the earth
was at the center of the universe would consume

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.260
Aristotle for the rest of his life.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:45.279
Even though he continued to teach the theories
of Ptolemy in his classes, he become more

00:14:45.279 --> 00:14:48.010
convinced that Copernicus was right.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.740
In 1597, he established written communication
with German astronomer Johannes Kepler who

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.220
was also an advocate of the Copernican theory.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:59.590
Kepler had published a work supporting Copernicus
and encouraged Galileo to do the same.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:04.210
However, the Italian was worried that doing
so would bring upon him the anger of the Catholic

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:09.010
church which held that the earth was undoubtedly
the center of the universe.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:12.350
On a visit to Venice, Galileo met a woman
by the name of Marina Gamba.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:15.830
A relationship developed, with Marina moving
in with him.

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:20.050
Before long a child was born to the unwed
couple, the first of three, two girls and

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.050
a boy.

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:25.410
In 1610, Galileo left Padua, taking his two
daughters (both given to the convent eventually

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:30.300
due to their illegitimate births) but leaving
his son, Vincenzo, with Marina.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:33.730
Years later Marina married another man, but
Galileo remained friendly with her throughout

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:36.310
his lifetime.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:42.930
Growing Fame
In the first decade of the 1600’s, Galileo

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:44.480
continued to invent new technologies.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:48.300
In 1604, he observed and discussed Kepler’s
supernova..

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:52.390
His talk was so popular that he had to move
from the overcrowded lecture theater to an

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:54.860
outside forum so that everyone could hear
him.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:58.910
In 1609, Galileo began working on a machine
that would magnify the night sky.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:04.250
He used two eyeglass lenses, one concave and
the other convex, placing them at opposite

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:05.980
ends of a long metal tube.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:10.540
When looking through this ‘Venetian glass’,
later called a telescope, the stars were magnified

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:11.649
several times.

00:16:11.649 --> 00:16:15.750
The invention became a hit with prominent
people from all over Italy clambering to get

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:20.210
a look through the amazing machine which allowed
you to see things that were far away.

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:25.470
His telescopes were also a profitable sideline
for Galileo, who sold them to merchants who

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:28.730
found them useful both at sea and as items
of trade.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:32.310
The invention reflected positively on the
University of Padua, prompting the board to

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:36.830
offer Galileo a lifetime professorship and
increase his salary to 1000 florins.

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:41.500
Before long, Galileo had improved his telescope
until it was able to magnify up to thirty

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:42.500
times.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.060
This enabled him to look through it at the
moon.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:48.160
He discovered that, rather being perfectly
round and smooth, as Aristotle had written,

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.290
it was rough and filled with craters and valleys.

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:56.430
His telescope also enabled Galileo to identify
and observe the moons of other planets.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:17:01.550
During the period between January 8 and 14,
1610 he identified that Jupiter actually had

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:02.720
4 moons and are now called Io, Europa, Ganymede,
and Callisto.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.679
Then in July of that same year he became the
first person to identify the rings around

00:17:06.679 --> 00:17:09.630
Saturn, though he didn’t quite realize what
they were at the time.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:14.279
Later that year he identified that Jupiter
and Mars had phases, similar to the moon.

00:17:14.279 --> 00:17:18.630
Through the telescope, Galileo was able to
identify that there were many, many more stars

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:24.110
than had originally been thought and, therefore,
that the universe was much larger than previously

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:25.110
thought.

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:28.669
This upset many people who thought that through
his telescope, Galileo was using some form

00:17:28.669 --> 00:17:30.929
of trickery to make his new discoveries.

00:17:30.929 --> 00:17:34.710
In 1609, he was invited to move to the palace
of the Grand Duke in Florence.

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:37.759
He accepted, moving back to his home region
of Tuscany.

00:17:37.759 --> 00:17:41.690
His job now involved giving lectures to princes
and other members of the royal family.

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:47.570
No longer having to lecture, he now had the
freedom to study and write books.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:54.840
Growing Opposition
By 1615, there was a rising tide of opposition

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:55.840
to Galileo.

00:17:55.840 --> 00:18:00.230
Many of his discoveries had flown in the face
of accepted Church doctrine, which was closely

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:02.389
aligned with the teachings of Aristotle.

00:18:02.389 --> 00:18:06.919
People began asserting that his findings were
at odds with the scriptures, which were the

00:18:06.919 --> 00:18:08.510
revealed word of God.

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:13.030
Galileo responded by writing that the Bible
was not a scientific textbook, but, where

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:16.470
it touched on science, it was, actually in
harmony with his findings.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:20.220
It was people who had interpreted the scriptures
incorrectly.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.919
The letter that Galileo wrote soon found its
way into the hands of his enemies, particularly

00:18:23.919 --> 00:18:25.600
the Dominicans of St. Mark.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.070
They viewed what he had written as heresy
and they made sure that a copy of the letter

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:30.919
got in front of the Pope in Rome.

00:18:30.919 --> 00:18:35.260
The pope called for the leading Dominican
to come and give testimony about Galilee.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:39.169
This man made the most of the opportunity,
telling lies to paint Galileo and his followers

00:18:39.169 --> 00:18:40.279
as blasphemers.

00:18:40.279 --> 00:18:42.309
Galileo was not cowered.

00:18:42.309 --> 00:18:47.139
He considered those who spoke against him
as ignorant fools and set out to defend himself.

00:18:47.139 --> 00:18:51.749
To do so, he traveled to the center of the
cauldron itself, Rome.

00:18:51.749 --> 00:18:57.499
He appeared before the Holy Office on February
26, 1616 and was ordered to never again teach

00:18:57.499 --> 00:18:59.929
that the sun was the center of the universe.

00:18:59.929 --> 00:19:03.600
His reasoned argumentation in his defense
fell on deaf ears.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:06.249
Galileo remained in Rome to argue with his
detractors.

00:19:06.249 --> 00:19:10.570
This was not pleasing to his patron back in
the court of Tuscany, who felt that the conflict

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:13.630
could cause a rift between them and Rome.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:17.460
In June, Galileo finally complied, returning
to Florence.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.649
The stress of defending his reputation had
taken a toll on Galileo.

00:19:20.649 --> 00:19:25.309
He was constantly plagued with physical pain
and spent increasing amounts of time bedridden.

00:19:25.309 --> 00:19:29.730
In 1617, he moved to a hilltop villa just
out of Florence in order to benefit from the

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:30.799
rural air.

00:19:30.799 --> 00:19:35.200
He continued to study the heavens but was
now far more guarded about what he wrote.

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.900
Then, in 1618, his ailments worsened.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:41.739
He was confined to bed for several weeks and
became extremely weak.

00:19:41.739 --> 00:19:44.549
By 1624, there was a new Pope, Urban VIII.

00:19:44.549 --> 00:19:49.070
As a cardinal he had expressed admiration
for Galilee, but had never agreed with the

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:50.070
Copernican theory.

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:53.619
A slightly recovered Galileo was convinced
to make a pilgrimage to Rome in order to meet

00:19:53.619 --> 00:19:56.440
with the Pope to assess his standing with
the pontiff.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:20:00.590
Urban received him well, even presenting a
medal and promising a pension.

00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:04.869
Galileo also noticed that church officials
around Rome were far less hostile to him than

00:20:04.869 --> 00:20:06.120
they had been.

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:10.119
He felt confident, believing that the new
Pope had eased the pressure on him.

00:20:10.119 --> 00:20:15.960
In 1629, Galileo wrote a masterful work, Dialogue
Concerning the Two Chief World Systems which

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:20.649
presented a dialogue between three men on
the merits of the Ptolemaic and Copernican

00:20:20.649 --> 00:20:21.649
theories.

00:20:21.649 --> 00:20:24.830
Before it could be published, he had to present
it to the chief censor of the press in Rome.

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:28.870
He was forced to make changes that presented
the Ptolemaic theory in the more favorable

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:34.710
light and to state clearly that the ideas
expressed were not facts but simply opinions.

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:39.100
Galileo made the changes, but a series of
delays prevented the book’s publication

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:43.279
until 1632.

00:20:43.279 --> 00:20:48.940
Inquisition
The new book brought out the ire of the enemies

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:50.980
of the now 68-year old Galileo.

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:53.710
When Pope Urban got to read it, he became
enraged.

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:56.929
He had convinced himself that one of the three
fictional debaters in the book was based on

00:20:56.929 --> 00:21:01.340
himself, and considered this to be the highest
insult imaginable.

00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:05.460
In August 1632, all copies of the book sold
in bookshops were seized and the printer was

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:06.789
ordered to cease publication.

00:21:06.789 --> 00:21:11.190
Then, in September, Galileo was ordered to
appear before the Holy Roman Inquisition.

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:15.030
After stalling due to ill health, he finally
headed to Rome to face his inquisitors on

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:17.690
January 20, 1633.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:21.549
He had to wait until April 12th to appear
before the vice-commissioner of the holy office

00:21:21.549 --> 00:21:24.700
and two other examiners for preliminary questioning.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:29.090
It was stated that his book was in direct
violation of the 1616 oath he had taken not

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:31.629
to publicly discuss the teachings of Copernicus.

00:21:31.629 --> 00:21:34.999
In his defense, Galileo contended that the
book was simply a dialogue between the two

00:21:34.999 --> 00:21:39.360
theories and did not favor the view that the
sun was at the center of the universe.

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:41.720
A second interview was held on April 30th.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:46.529
This time Galileo was more conciliatory, agreeing
that the arguments in favor of the Corpunican

00:21:46.529 --> 00:21:47.529
theory.

00:21:47.529 --> 00:21:49.700
. .
come to the ear of the reader with far greater

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:55.360
force and power than should have been imparted
to them by someone who regarded them as inconclusive.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:59.580
This interview concluded with the examiners
agreeing to give Galileo time to prepare his

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:00.609
full defense.

00:22:00.609 --> 00:22:03.399
That hearing was held on June 21st.

00:22:03.399 --> 00:22:07.600
Before he could fully develop his defense,
he was asked point blank whether he had ever

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:09.009
held to the Copernican theory.

00:22:09.009 --> 00:22:14.080
He answered that he had initially been indifferent
to both theories, but after careful study,

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:17.029
now believed in the wisdom of the Ptolemaic
theory.

00:22:17.029 --> 00:22:20.999
This was clearly not true and none of the
examiners believed him.

00:22:20.999 --> 00:22:25.309
The following day he was sentenced to a prison
term, to be decided later and his book was

00:22:25.309 --> 00:22:26.309
banned.

00:22:26.309 --> 00:22:29.559
He was then mde to read a statement of apology
on bended knee.

00:22:29.559 --> 00:22:32.240
Pope Urban seemed satisfied with this outcome.

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.700
Galileo had been both condemned and humiliated.

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:38.700
The pope decided that the imprisonment to
which Galileo had been sentenced could be

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:43.669
carried out by way of home detention at the
grand duke of Tuscany’s villa near Rome.

00:22:43.669 --> 00:22:47.299
However, Galileo was desperate to live out
his last years in his Florence, where his

00:22:47.299 --> 00:22:48.299
family were located.

00:22:48.299 --> 00:22:51.629
He wrote to the pope begging this indulgence,
which was granted.

00:22:51.629 --> 00:22:54.330
He would be confined to the villa of his powerful
friend, Ascanio Piccolomini.

00:22:54.330 --> 00:23:00.490
After five months he was permitted to return
to his own villa at Arcetri.

00:23:00.490 --> 00:23:05.820
The Last Years
Galileo’s final years were marked by ill-health.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.739
By 1637, he was nearly totally blind.

00:23:08.739 --> 00:23:13.170
Before his sight was gone completely, he managed
to complete his last and, in his view, most

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:18.609
outstanding work, entitled Discourses and
Mathematical Demonstrations of Two New Sciences.

00:23:18.609 --> 00:23:22.110
Under the rules of his sentence he could not
publish it in Italy and so the manuscript

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.720
was smuggled to Amsterdam and published there.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:28.049
During his last days, the blind genius received
many visitors to his villa at Acetri.

00:23:28.049 --> 00:23:32.190
He died on January 8, 1642 at the age of 77.

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:35.220
He had been fighting fever and heart problems
for months.

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:38.879
Nearly a hundred years later, with his brilliance
having finally been recognized, his remains

00:23:38.879 --> 00:23:43.630
were removed from the chapel at Santa Croce
and relocated to a much more elaborate mausoleum

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:45.109
inside the church.

00:23:45.109 --> 00:23:48.890
During this move, three fingers and a tooth
were removed from his remains.

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:53.039
One of these fingers, the middle finger from
Galileo's right hand, is currently on exhibition

00:23:53.039 --> 00:23:55.980
at the Museo Galileo in Florence, Italy.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:24:01.340
A monument was also erected with a bust of
Galileo and two figures representing his monumental

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:24.559
achievements in the worlds of astronomy

00:24:24.559 --> 00:24:27.949
and geometry.

